WEBVTT

1
00:00:13.931 --> 00:00:15.411
In Search of Dharma.

2
00:00:17.064 --> 00:00:19.691
7: Dharmas in a Secular Age.

3
00:00:21.851 --> 00:00:25.991
Hundreds of millions of people now say
they are "spiritual but not religious."

4
00:00:26.391 --> 00:00:28.411
That is not the absence of a dharma.

5
00:00:28.951 --> 00:00:30.491
It is the search for one,

6
00:00:30.551 --> 00:00:34.871
carried out by people who have lost the
old foundations but kept the old need.

7
00:00:36.891 --> 00:00:41.371
There is a kind of person nearly everyone
now knows, and many of us have been.

8
00:00:43.408 --> 00:00:47.231
She left the religion she was raised in,
or was never really given one,

9
00:00:47.351 --> 00:00:50.191
and she does not believe in much that she
cannot see.

10
00:00:50.671 --> 00:00:53.411
But she is not empty, and she is not
satisfied.

11
00:00:53.907 --> 00:00:55.711
So she has assembled something.

12
00:00:56.211 --> 00:01:00.111
A little yoga, for the body and for
whatever the body seems to know.

13
00:01:00.711 --> 00:01:04.251
A meditation app on her phone, ten minutes
most mornings,

14
00:01:04.251 --> 00:01:08.311
its closing bell followed at once by the
notifications waiting behind it.

15
00:01:08.931 --> 00:01:11.331
A book of Stoic sayings on the nightstand,

16
00:01:11.431 --> 00:01:14.271
a few wise voices in her headphones on the
commute,

17
00:01:14.391 --> 00:01:16.831
some hard-won vocabulary from therapy,

18
00:01:17.031 --> 00:01:19.091
perhaps a quiet practice of gratitude she

19
00:01:19.091 --> 00:01:21.351
would feel slightly embarrassed to
describe.

20
00:01:21.791 --> 00:01:23.371
She has not joined anything.

21
00:01:24.036 --> 00:01:25.931
She has built a private patchwork,

22
00:01:25.931 --> 00:01:29.871
stitched together from half a dozen
traditions she does not formally belong to,

23
00:01:29.871 --> 00:01:31.931
and she lives by it as best she can.

24
00:01:33.731 --> 00:01:35.971
There are now a vast number of these
people,

25
00:01:35.971 --> 00:01:40.471
and the surveys have a name for them: the
"nones," the religiously unaffiliated.

26
00:01:41.191 --> 00:01:43.191
Theirs is the category that grew faster

27
00:01:43.191 --> 00:01:48.571
than any other in the Western world across
the two decades to about 2020 (in the US,

28
00:01:48.571 --> 00:01:56.631
from 16% of adults in 2007 to roughly 29%
by 2024), before its rise levelled off.

29
00:01:57.291 --> 00:02:00.451
The usual way to read them is as a story
of decline,

30
00:02:00.451 --> 00:02:05.251
the slow draining-away of religion, leaving
behind a thin consumer spirituality,

31
00:02:05.351 --> 00:02:06.631
a supermarket of the self

32
00:02:06.631 --> 00:02:10.251
where lonely people push trolleys past the
incense and the self-help.

33
00:02:10.611 --> 00:02:12.071
I read them differently.

34
00:02:12.541 --> 00:02:16.911
The "spiritual but not religious" seeker
is not the absence of a dharma.

35
00:02:17.651 --> 00:02:21.451
It is the search for one, conducted in the
hardest possible conditions,

36
00:02:21.451 --> 00:02:25.031
by someone who has inherited the deep
human need to hold a life together

37
00:02:25.031 --> 00:02:29.211
but has lost every one of the old foundations
on which a dharma used to be built.

38
00:02:31.112 --> 00:02:33.691
The old dharmas, with all their captures
and cages,

39
00:02:33.691 --> 00:02:35.691
were made for a world that is gone,

40
00:02:35.711 --> 00:02:39.051
the world of the village and the empire
and the believing crowd.

41
00:02:39.671 --> 00:02:43.211
For hundreds of millions of people the
gods have fallen silent,

42
00:02:43.291 --> 00:02:45.371
and the village has become the internet.

43
00:02:45.971 --> 00:02:50.011
So the question now is whether a dharma
can survive that loss at all.

44
00:02:50.571 --> 00:02:54.651
Can there be a real one, a binding one, a
transforming one,

45
00:02:54.731 --> 00:02:58.291
without the foundations on which every
previous dharma stood?

46
00:03:01.317 --> 00:03:03.651
Three things a dharma used to get for free

47
00:03:06.433 --> 00:03:07.771
To answer that, we have

48
00:03:07.771 --> 00:03:11.031
to be specific about what those lost
foundations actually did,

49
00:03:11.031 --> 00:03:13.411
because "faith" is too vague.

50
00:03:13.823 --> 00:03:17.951
A traditional religious dharma got three
things, more or less for free,

51
00:03:17.951 --> 00:03:21.031
that a secular one has to find some other
way to earn.

52
00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:24.211
Free, that is, to the person born inside
it;

53
00:03:24.211 --> 00:03:27.091
the tradition itself paid for them
continuously,

54
00:03:27.091 --> 00:03:29.631
in institutions and labour and discipline,

55
00:03:29.671 --> 00:03:32.691
and in no small amount of exclusion and
coercion.

56
00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:34.871
The inheritance was free.

57
00:03:35.231 --> 00:03:36.711
The estate never was.

58
00:03:38.644 --> 00:03:40.311
The first was grounding:

59
00:03:40.371 --> 00:03:43.871
a reason its commands were binding rather
than merely optional,

60
00:03:44.051 --> 00:03:48.811
usually a commanding God who made "thou
shalt" into law rather than a preference.

61
00:03:50.031 --> 00:03:53.871
The second was installation: the whole
apparatus of ritual,

62
00:03:53.951 --> 00:03:56.111
sacred ordeal, and tight community,

63
00:03:56.111 --> 00:03:58.351
anchored to unfalsifiable belief,

64
00:03:58.351 --> 00:04:01.671
that drove a dharma down into people until
it ran their lives.

65
00:04:02.491 --> 00:04:06.971
And the third was simply a world that
cooperated, a slow, face-to-face,

66
00:04:06.971 --> 00:04:09.691
information-poor world in which the main
obstacle

67
00:04:09.711 --> 00:04:12.491
to living well was your own untrained
heart.

68
00:04:12.811 --> 00:04:16.731
A secular dharma in the twenty-first
century has lost all three.

69
00:04:17.351 --> 00:04:22.911
It has no divine lawgiver, a frayed and
improvised apparatus, and a new world that,

70
00:04:22.911 --> 00:04:26.131
far from being indifferent to the
holding-together a dharma does,

71
00:04:26.131 --> 00:04:28.091
is actively engineered against it.

72
00:04:30.151 --> 00:04:32.731
Before testing whether that loss can be
survived,

73
00:04:32.731 --> 00:04:37.711
the frame itself has to stand trial, because
the story I have just told has a name,

74
00:04:37.711 --> 00:04:38.491
and the philosopher

75
00:04:38.491 --> 00:04:42.871
whose title this part borrows spent nine
hundred pages arguing against it.

76
00:04:43.831 --> 00:04:47.591
Charles Taylor calls accounts like mine
subtraction stories:

77
00:04:47.791 --> 00:04:52.211
tales in which modernity is simply what
remains once human beings have "lost,

78
00:04:52.211 --> 00:04:56.691
or sloughed off, or liberated themselves
from" the old confining illusions.

79
00:04:57.290 --> 00:04:59.231
His objection is not a quibble.

80
00:04:59.971 --> 00:05:03.831
Western secularity, he argues, is "the
fruit of new inventions,

81
00:05:04.111 --> 00:05:07.451
newly constructed self-understandings and
related practices";

82
00:05:08.171 --> 00:05:11.251
not an emptiness left by religion's
retreat but a new,

83
00:05:11.251 --> 00:05:12.751
positive, built condition,

84
00:05:13.251 --> 00:05:16.971
whose signature invention is what he calls
exclusive humanism,

85
00:05:17.191 --> 00:05:22.031
the first widely available way of living
oriented entirely within this world.

86
00:05:22.711 --> 00:05:27.331
The anthropologist Talal Asad presses the
same point from another flank:

87
00:05:27.451 --> 00:05:31.151
"the secular" is not the neutral ground
left when the gods withdraw;

88
00:05:31.151 --> 00:05:34.231
it is a constructed formation with a
history of its own.

89
00:05:35.911 --> 00:05:38.171
If they are right, and I think they are,

90
00:05:38.191 --> 00:05:42.431
then my talk of silence and loss is the
believer's-eye view of something that looks,

91
00:05:42.491 --> 00:05:44.951
from the other side, like a construction
site.

92
00:05:45.531 --> 00:05:48.831
This does not weaken my argument; I think
it completes it.

93
00:05:49.291 --> 00:05:52.411
The secular age is a crowded workshop of
new dharmas,

94
00:05:52.771 --> 00:05:54.751
exclusive humanism first among them,

95
00:05:54.811 --> 00:05:57.791
the proliferating "nova" of options Taylor
describes,

96
00:05:57.911 --> 00:06:01.471
and the workshop is exactly where the
whole of the next part takes place.

97
00:06:03.931 --> 00:06:05.931
But Taylor sharpens the problem too,

98
00:06:05.931 --> 00:06:09.051
in a way my subtraction telling actually
understated.

99
00:06:09.611 --> 00:06:13.991
In the old world, the three free gifts
went to whoever was born inside the dharma.

100
00:06:14.511 --> 00:06:19.671
In the secular age, Taylor argues, every
position, belief and unbelief alike,

101
00:06:19.671 --> 00:06:24.951
is held as one option among others:
contested, fragile, reflectively chosen.

102
00:06:25.471 --> 00:06:28.651
Which means the believer has lost the free
gifts as well.

103
00:06:29.191 --> 00:06:33.951
Nobody now inherits grounding,
installation, and a cooperating world;

104
00:06:34.231 --> 00:06:39.391
every dharma in the nova, religious or
secular, has to earn all three the hard way.

105
00:06:39.811 --> 00:06:44.191
The tests that follow are therefore not
the secular dharma's special burden.

106
00:06:44.571 --> 00:06:47.351
They are the entrance exam every dharma
now sits,

107
00:06:47.431 --> 00:06:50.351
and the secular candidate merely sits it
without notes.

108
00:06:53.511 --> 00:06:54.611
Can it bind?

109
00:06:56.931 --> 00:06:59.591
Grounding is the objection the believer
presses hardest,

110
00:06:59.671 --> 00:07:01.531
and the question Part 1 raised.

111
00:07:02.011 --> 00:07:06.871
Without a God behind it, the challenge
runs, a dharma cannot really bind anyone.

112
00:07:07.651 --> 00:07:09.851
"Thou shalt not" needs a lawgiver;

113
00:07:10.011 --> 00:07:14.111
remove the divine legislator and all our
fine moral language collapses

114
00:07:14.211 --> 00:07:16.411
into "I would rather people didn't."

115
00:07:16.751 --> 00:07:20.511
The philosopher Elizabeth Anscombe put it
in sharp terms:

116
00:07:20.631 --> 00:07:25.691
the whole vocabulary of moral obligation,
of what one ought and must do,

117
00:07:25.691 --> 00:07:29.151
is the wreckage of a system that assumed a
divine law,

118
00:07:29.451 --> 00:07:33.431
and it becomes literally incoherent once
the lawgiver is gone,

119
00:07:33.451 --> 00:07:36.271
leaving only preferences dressed up as
duties.

120
00:07:38.031 --> 00:07:41.751
It is a serious challenge, and the answer
comes in two moves,

121
00:07:41.911 --> 00:07:45.811
the first of which Part 1 named and left
standing.

122
00:07:46.371 --> 00:07:48.831
Plato's old trap, the Euthyphro question:

123
00:07:48.991 --> 00:07:53.851
is a thing good because God commands it,
or does God command it because it is good?

124
00:07:54.331 --> 00:07:58.471
Take the first horn, and morality is
arbitrary, a divine whim.

125
00:07:58.911 --> 00:08:02.731
Take the second, and the goodness was
already there, independent,

126
00:08:02.731 --> 00:08:06.411
for God to recognise, so God is not its
source after all.

127
00:08:06.831 --> 00:08:09.451
Either way, the divine command was never
really doing

128
00:08:09.451 --> 00:08:12.591
the grounding work it advertised itself as
doing.

129
00:08:12.991 --> 00:08:15.391
The believer who feels she stands on
bedrock,

130
00:08:15.391 --> 00:08:19.291
and pities the secularist standing on
sand, has mistaken the view.

131
00:08:19.731 --> 00:08:21.671
She was never on bedrock either.

132
00:08:23.251 --> 00:08:26.431
The second move is harder, and it cuts
against everyone,

133
00:08:26.431 --> 00:08:29.311
the believer and the secularist and the
scientist alike.

134
00:08:30.011 --> 00:08:32.571
There is an old problem, sometimes called
the trilemma,

135
00:08:33.131 --> 00:08:36.371
about any attempt to justify something all
the way down:

136
00:08:36.431 --> 00:08:39.991
every chain of "but why?" must end in one
of three places,

137
00:08:40.251 --> 00:08:45.131
an infinite regress, a circle, or simply a
place to stop and plant your feet.

138
00:08:45.311 --> 00:08:46.931
There is no exception.

139
00:08:47.311 --> 00:08:50.991
No system of ethics, religious, secular,
or scientific,

140
00:08:50.991 --> 00:08:54.811
has ever reached genuine bedrock, because
there is no bedrock to reach;

141
00:08:55.151 --> 00:08:56.571
the demand for an ultimate,

142
00:08:56.571 --> 00:09:00.691
unanswerable foundation is a demand that
nothing in human life can meet.

143
00:09:01.781 --> 00:09:06.151
And once that is seen, the challenge to
secular dharmas loses its force,

144
00:09:06.151 --> 00:09:10.391
because it was holding the secularist to a
standard the believer never met either.

145
00:09:10.871 --> 00:09:15.531
The honest question was never "is it
grounded all the way down," since nothing is.

146
00:09:16.151 --> 00:09:19.591
It is "is it grounded well enough to live
by, and how?"

147
00:09:21.051 --> 00:09:25.911
And to that question a secular dharma has
real answers, just not cosmic ones.

148
00:09:26.351 --> 00:09:28.751
It can ground its claim on us in the brute
fact

149
00:09:28.751 --> 00:09:31.831
that we are the kind of creature to whom
things already matter,

150
00:09:31.911 --> 00:09:36.871
who cannot, except by damage, stop caring
about suffering, our own and others'.

151
00:09:37.411 --> 00:09:41.171
It can ground it in what the philosopher
Philippa Foot called natural goodness,

152
00:09:41.271 --> 00:09:43.491
the thought that for a living thing of a
particular

153
00:09:43.491 --> 00:09:46.751
kind there is a recognisable shape to
flourishing,

154
00:09:46.751 --> 00:09:48.831
the way there is for an oak or a wolf;

155
00:09:49.151 --> 00:09:53.451
cruelty and cowardice are defects in a
human life as real as blight in a tree.

156
00:09:53.822 --> 00:09:57.171
It can ground it in the moral sentiments
we are simply built with,

157
00:09:57.231 --> 00:09:58.971
the empathy and the sense of fairness

158
00:09:58.971 --> 00:10:02.491
that make another's pain register in us
whether we consent or not.

159
00:10:03.051 --> 00:10:05.251
None of these is a thunderbolt from
heaven.

160
00:10:05.651 --> 00:10:06.971
Each is enough to live by.

161
00:10:07.651 --> 00:10:09.731
The binding they produce is real,

162
00:10:09.771 --> 00:10:12.691
but it is a different shape of binding
than the commandment:

163
00:10:12.711 --> 00:10:15.131
it is internal rather than imposed,

164
00:10:15.311 --> 00:10:20.291
conditional rather than absolute, a matter
of if we would live well with others,

165
00:10:20.331 --> 00:10:23.331
then, rather than thou shalt, or else.

166
00:10:23.851 --> 00:10:28.571
That conditional, this-worldly binding may
be the only kind there ever was.

167
00:10:29.151 --> 00:10:32.831
The commandment-shaped certainty the
believer mourns was, perhaps,

168
00:10:33.011 --> 00:10:36.211
always a story we told about a binding
that actually came

169
00:10:36.211 --> 00:10:38.711
from the same place the secularist's does:

170
00:10:38.911 --> 00:10:42.131
the fact that we are an animal to whom it
matters how we live.

171
00:10:43.911 --> 00:10:47.131
That conditional shape is easy to mistake
for weakness.

172
00:10:47.811 --> 00:10:49.491
To say a dharma binds you only

173
00:10:49.491 --> 00:10:52.851
if you would live well is not to say it
binds you feebly.

174
00:10:53.431 --> 00:10:54.891
Compare the rules of health.

175
00:10:55.451 --> 00:10:58.751
They are not handed down from the sky;
they simply hold,

176
00:10:58.751 --> 00:11:01.711
conditionally, for any creature that wants
to go on living:

177
00:11:01.831 --> 00:11:06.191
if you would not sicken, then eat and move
and sleep in roughly these ways.

178
00:11:06.791 --> 00:11:11.491
Nobody calls the laws of nutrition flimsy
for being conditional on wanting to live.

179
00:11:12.211 --> 00:11:14.631
The dharmic "ought" works the same way,

180
00:11:14.631 --> 00:11:16.831
and it binds with real force on anyone

181
00:11:16.831 --> 00:11:20.531
who has not somehow opted out of caring
how their one life goes,

182
00:11:20.551 --> 00:11:23.351
which is to say on very nearly everyone
alive.

183
00:11:25.331 --> 00:11:28.171
The hardest version of the grounding
objection, though,

184
00:11:28.191 --> 00:11:31.951
comes not from the believer but from
inside the godless camp.

185
00:11:32.771 --> 00:11:36.551
Friedrich Nietzsche agreed entirely that
the divine ground was an illusion --

186
00:11:36.791 --> 00:11:40.391
and drew the opposite conclusion from the
one this part has been building.

187
00:11:41.331 --> 00:11:46.311
When the belief goes, he argued, the
morality raised on it must eventually follow:

188
00:11:46.471 --> 00:11:50.131
the secular moralists of his day had rid
themselves of the Christian God

189
00:11:50.711 --> 00:11:52.851
while clinging all the harder to Christian
morality,

190
00:11:54.431 --> 00:11:57.111
not seeing that a faith is a whole thought
out together,

191
00:11:57.111 --> 00:11:59.451
so that breaking out the God breaks the
rest.

192
00:12:01.511 --> 00:12:05.371
On his diagnosis the very things a secular
ethic keeps --

193
00:12:05.371 --> 00:12:08.411
compassion, the priority of suffering,

194
00:12:08.451 --> 00:12:13.071
care for the weak -- are not self-evident
truths but inherited residue,

195
00:12:13.351 --> 00:12:16.451
a pity-morality running on borrowed
authority;

196
00:12:16.990 --> 00:12:19.611
he even jeered that Europe's spreading
religion of

197
00:12:19.611 --> 00:12:23.011
sympathy was heading toward "a European
Buddhism".

198
00:12:23.491 --> 00:12:25.331
The taunt lands close to home,

199
00:12:25.331 --> 00:12:27.871
and it deserves a straight answer rather
than a wince.

200
00:12:28.211 --> 00:12:31.871
The answer is the one this book has been
assembling since Part 2:

201
00:12:32.191 --> 00:12:34.431
care is not the residue of any creed.

202
00:12:34.951 --> 00:12:37.251
The mattering, the empathy,

203
00:12:37.251 --> 00:12:40.931
the fairness-sense are older than every
scripture that later claimed them,

204
00:12:41.111 --> 00:12:43.851
the evolved endowment of a cooperative
animal,

205
00:12:43.911 --> 00:12:47.451
which the creeds inherited and codified
rather than invented.

206
00:12:48.251 --> 00:12:51.611
Nietzsche is owed one concession, and it
is a real one:

207
00:12:51.611 --> 00:12:55.511
he is right that the content cannot simply
be kept on as obvious.

208
00:12:56.071 --> 00:12:59.391
It must be defended, positively, on its
own this-worldly ground.

209
00:13:01.533 --> 00:13:05.171
And a dharma can add an answer here that
is peculiarly its own.

210
00:13:05.591 --> 00:13:09.031
It does not finally rest its authority on
an argument you could win

211
00:13:09.031 --> 00:13:10.471
or lose in a debate.

212
00:13:10.872 --> 00:13:13.991
It rests it on an invitation: come and
see.

213
00:13:14.371 --> 00:13:18.951
The old Buddhist word is ehipassiko, and
what it asks for is your experiment.

214
00:13:19.451 --> 00:13:22.331
Live this way, it says, actually do the
practice,

215
00:13:22.631 --> 00:13:24.651
and watch what happens to your own
suffering

216
00:13:24.777 --> 00:13:26.751
and to the suffering of the people around
you.

217
00:13:27.371 --> 00:13:31.211
The grounding, on this view, is a result
discovered inside a life,

218
00:13:31.211 --> 00:13:35.091
and that relocates the whole question from
the heavens down to lived consequence,

219
00:13:35.191 --> 00:13:38.031
which is exactly where a secular age can
still test it.

220
00:13:38.631 --> 00:13:40.971
It is a humbler foundation than a
commandment,

221
00:13:40.971 --> 00:13:43.031
but it is also one you can actually check.

222
00:13:46.071 --> 00:13:47.451
Can it install?

223
00:13:49.536 --> 00:13:53.191
Grounding, though, is the easier of the
two philosophical problems,

224
00:13:53.271 --> 00:13:56.251
and the one secular dharmas worry about
too much.

225
00:13:56.831 --> 00:14:00.771
The harder one is installation, and it is
where most of them fail.

226
00:14:02.551 --> 00:14:07.331
A dharma gets inside a person not by
argument but by drilling: by ritual,

227
00:14:07.331 --> 00:14:09.771
repeated practice, and tight community,

228
00:14:09.831 --> 00:14:12.691
driven below conscious thought until it
runs a life.

229
00:14:13.471 --> 00:14:16.511
And part of what made that drilling stick,
historically,

230
00:14:16.511 --> 00:14:20.891
was that it was anchored to claims one
could never test and so never disprove.

231
00:14:21.511 --> 00:14:25.431
A secular dharma throws the unfalsifiable
anchor overboard on purpose.

232
00:14:26.231 --> 00:14:29.731
The question is whether the apparatus
still works once that is done,

233
00:14:29.731 --> 00:14:32.911
and the answer is that it sometimes does
and often does not,

234
00:14:32.911 --> 00:14:34.711
and the difference between the two
outcomes

235
00:14:34.711 --> 00:14:37.271
is the most practical thing a seeker can
learn.

236
00:14:39.131 --> 00:14:40.511
Take an ancient practice,

237
00:14:40.791 --> 00:14:44.991
lift the one technique out of the demanding
ethical and communal whole it grew in,

238
00:14:45.091 --> 00:14:48.611
and sell that technique on its own as a
tool for feeling better.

239
00:14:49.871 --> 00:14:51.571
This is what happened to mindfulness.

240
00:14:52.831 --> 00:14:56.471
Pulled out of Buddhism and packaged as an
eight-week stress-reduction course,

241
00:14:56.471 --> 00:14:59.131
it spread to clinics and corporations and
schools,

242
00:14:59.191 --> 00:15:01.531
and on its own narrow terms it works:

243
00:15:01.751 --> 00:15:06.091
the careful studies find real if modest
reductions in anxiety and pain.

244
00:15:07.051 --> 00:15:10.131
There is nothing wrong with a clinical
tool doing clinical work;

245
00:15:10.251 --> 00:15:13.371
nobody faults an analgesic for not being a
way of life.

246
00:15:14.371 --> 00:15:17.071
But as a dharma it was hollowed out in the
lifting,

247
00:15:17.111 --> 00:15:20.571
which is why the nickname "McMindfulness,"
has bitten so hard.

248
00:15:21.811 --> 00:15:24.451
Stripped of the ethics that once gave it a
direction,

249
00:15:24.451 --> 00:15:26.711
mindfulness became a way of helping
stressed

250
00:15:26.711 --> 00:15:29.031
and exploited people tolerate the
conditions

251
00:15:29.031 --> 00:15:31.051
that were stressing and exploiting them,

252
00:15:31.451 --> 00:15:35.391
a tool for making a worker more productive
rather than a person more free.

253
00:15:35.931 --> 00:15:40.131
The lesson is brutal and clear: efficacy
is not transformation.

254
00:15:40.731 --> 00:15:45.091
A practice can be clinically proven to
work and have entirely lost the point.

255
00:15:46.897 --> 00:15:51.171
But the other outcome is real, and it is
why this is not a counsel of despair.

256
00:15:52.031 --> 00:15:54.791
One can strip the metaphysics and keep the
whole bundle,

257
00:15:54.791 --> 00:15:57.591
the practice and the ethics and the
community together,

258
00:15:57.631 --> 00:15:59.651
and put something this-worldly in the
place

259
00:15:59.651 --> 00:16:02.071
the old supernatural anchor used to hold.

260
00:16:02.771 --> 00:16:06.591
This is what the former monk Stephen
Batchelor calls secular Buddhism,

261
00:16:06.611 --> 00:16:08.631
or better, secular dharma.

262
00:16:09.231 --> 00:16:11.511
He lets the rebirth and the karma fall
away

263
00:16:11.511 --> 00:16:14.051
as the cultural packaging of ancient
India,

264
00:16:14.051 --> 00:16:17.411
and keeps the contemplative practice, but,
crucially,

265
00:16:17.411 --> 00:16:19.871
he does not keep only the soothing
technique.

266
00:16:20.571 --> 00:16:23.591
He deliberately puts the ethics back, and
widens them,

267
00:16:23.871 --> 00:16:26.071
insisting that the practice must turn on
racism

268
00:16:26.071 --> 00:16:30.331
and injustice in the world as much as on
greed and craving in the self,

269
00:16:30.511 --> 00:16:33.431
exactly the social dimension that
McMindfulness had dropped.

270
00:16:35.291 --> 00:16:39.571
One doctrine in that discarded packaging
deserves a second look before it goes,

271
00:16:39.611 --> 00:16:44.811
because the rule being applied -- reconstruct,
don't extract -- applies to it too.

272
00:16:45.391 --> 00:16:48.451
Karma can be let fall with the rebirth it
was wired to,

273
00:16:48.451 --> 00:16:50.671
or indicted: wedded to caste,

274
00:16:50.851 --> 00:16:52.751
the doctrine that told a man his station

275
00:16:52.751 --> 00:16:55.311
was his own past deeds collecting their
debt,

276
00:16:55.371 --> 00:16:58.531
until Ambedkar led half a million people
out from under it.

277
00:16:59.091 --> 00:17:01.971
But there is a third option the argument
should at least name:

278
00:17:02.591 --> 00:17:06.911
karma naturalised, as a this-worldly
technology of intention and consequence.

279
00:17:07.961 --> 00:17:10.771
The Buddha himself defined karma as
intention,

280
00:17:10.951 --> 00:17:14.451
and read that way the doctrine asserts
things a secular ethic can keep

281
00:17:14.451 --> 00:17:15.571
with a straight face:

282
00:17:15.791 --> 00:17:20.031
that intentions shape the actor who holds
them, each choice grooving the next,

283
00:17:20.291 --> 00:17:24.131
which is Part 5's habituation machinery in
ancient dress;

284
00:17:24.551 --> 00:17:28.951
that consequences propagate through a
community whether or not anyone is watching,

285
00:17:28.951 --> 00:17:31.971
which is Part 2's reputation machinery
likewise;

286
00:17:32.291 --> 00:17:34.731
and that no ledger is kept anywhere else.

287
00:17:35.451 --> 00:17:38.851
Rebirth was the packaging; the feedback
loop was the point.

288
00:17:39.461 --> 00:17:42.211
I flag this reconstruction rather than
perform it,

289
00:17:42.251 --> 00:17:44.651
since performing it would be a book of its
own.

290
00:17:45.531 --> 00:17:49.151
But a secular dharma that admires
Batchelor's method should notice

291
00:17:49.151 --> 00:17:52.151
that the method does not have to stop
where Batchelor stopped.

292
00:17:53.911 --> 00:17:56.971
Or look at the quiet success story of
modern Stoicism,

293
00:17:56.971 --> 00:17:59.831
which translated so cleanly into the
secular world

294
00:17:59.891 --> 00:18:02.431
that it literally became a school of
psychotherapy:

295
00:18:02.631 --> 00:18:07.091
the founders of cognitive behavioural
therapy openly credited Epictetus.

296
00:18:07.801 --> 00:18:10.571
His line "men are disturbed not by things,

297
00:18:10.571 --> 00:18:14.251
but by the views which they take of them"
is the seed of the whole method.

298
00:18:14.791 --> 00:18:19.731
The historian of ancient philosophy Pierre
Hadot reframes the entire project.

299
00:18:20.571 --> 00:18:24.251
Ancient philosophy, he showed, was never
mainly a set of doctrines;

300
00:18:24.291 --> 00:18:28.731
it was a way of life, a body of spiritual
exercises practised daily,

301
00:18:28.731 --> 00:18:31.371
and that practical core withered only
later,

302
00:18:31.691 --> 00:18:35.591
when philosophy hardened into something
merely read and argued about.

303
00:18:36.211 --> 00:18:37.431
On Hadot's reading,

304
00:18:37.571 --> 00:18:41.611
secular translation at its best is not a
theft from religion at all.

305
00:18:42.231 --> 00:18:45.551
It is a recovery, a restoring of the
lived, practised,

306
00:18:45.891 --> 00:18:48.371
transforming core that the traditions
themselves

307
00:18:48.411 --> 00:18:51.391
had often buried under doctrine and
institution.

308
00:18:53.371 --> 00:18:56.171
Secular translation has a transferable
structure rather

309
00:18:56.171 --> 00:18:58.391
than being one tradition's lucky accident:

310
00:18:58.431 --> 00:19:01.911
the very same operation has been performed
inside Christianity.

311
00:19:02.641 --> 00:19:07.771
The Anglican theologian Don Cupitt, in a
book bravely titled Taking Leave of God,

312
00:19:07.791 --> 00:19:11.891
argued that religious language can carry
our deepest human and ethical aspirations

313
00:19:11.911 --> 00:19:15.191
without pointing to any supernatural being
out there at all,

314
00:19:15.671 --> 00:19:17.091
sustaining a this-worldly,

315
00:19:17.091 --> 00:19:21.331
practised spirituality with the
metaphysics politely set to one side.

316
00:19:22.091 --> 00:19:24.891
Strip the supernatural, keep the practice
and the ethics,

317
00:19:25.111 --> 00:19:27.131
re-ground the whole thing in this life:

318
00:19:27.231 --> 00:19:30.811
it is precisely Batchelor's recipe cooked
in a different kitchen,

319
00:19:30.811 --> 00:19:33.431
and the two men recognised each other as
kin.

320
00:19:35.271 --> 00:19:39.111
From the successes and the failures
together, a clear rule emerges.

321
00:19:39.791 --> 00:19:44.371
A secular translation keeps its transforming
power when it does three things:

322
00:19:44.651 --> 00:19:47.351
it preserves the whole bundle of practice,
ethics,

323
00:19:47.351 --> 00:19:50.551
and community rather than lifting out the
technique alone;

324
00:19:50.951 --> 00:19:53.131
it supplies some this-worldly replacement

325
00:19:53.131 --> 00:19:55.491
for whatever the old metaphysics used to
do,

326
00:19:55.491 --> 00:19:57.891
the binding and the motivating and the
enduring;

327
00:19:58.031 --> 00:20:02.711
and it stays a way of life one practises
rather than a worldview one merely holds.

328
00:20:03.511 --> 00:20:06.731
Do all three and a dharma can survive the
loss of its gods.

329
00:20:07.271 --> 00:20:11.311
Do none of them, keeping the soothing
technique and discarding the rest,

330
00:20:11.311 --> 00:20:13.891
and the result is simply another
McMindfulness.

331
00:20:15.691 --> 00:20:17.591
The bundle has a third strand, though,

332
00:20:17.591 --> 00:20:20.591
and it is the one the modern world is
least able to supply.

333
00:20:21.451 --> 00:20:25.391
A dharma installs through community,
through a real and present "we":

334
00:20:25.391 --> 00:20:29.391
the congregation that drills the practice
together and holds its members to it,

335
00:20:29.431 --> 00:20:32.391
the people whose watching eyes make the
commitment cost something.

336
00:20:33.071 --> 00:20:35.251
And community is exactly what the secular

337
00:20:35.251 --> 00:20:37.811
and the digital age have thinned almost to
nothing.

338
00:20:38.611 --> 00:20:41.411
The self that modern life produces is what
its

339
00:20:41.411 --> 00:20:44.651
communitarian critics call the
unencumbered individual,

340
00:20:44.811 --> 00:20:47.231
cut loose from the dense web of
relationships

341
00:20:47.291 --> 00:20:49.511
that older dharmas simply took for
granted,

342
00:20:49.631 --> 00:20:53.611
and increasingly that self meets other
selves only through a screen.

343
00:20:54.051 --> 00:20:56.171
Our seeker with her app and her book is
trying

344
00:20:56.171 --> 00:20:58.691
to install a dharma essentially alone,

345
00:20:58.691 --> 00:21:02.371
which is a little like trying to learn a
language with no one to speak it to.

346
00:21:02.551 --> 00:21:06.871
She can keep the practice on her own, and
even, with effort, the ethics.

347
00:21:07.671 --> 00:21:10.971
The "we" is the hardest part to rebuild
from scratch,

348
00:21:11.151 --> 00:21:13.131
and a dharma without a "we" struggles

349
00:21:13.671 --> 00:21:16.371
to get beyond the surface of a single
private life.

350
00:21:16.731 --> 00:21:19.031
This, more than the missing God,

351
00:21:19.031 --> 00:21:22.611
may be the secular dharma's deepest
practical wound.

352
00:21:24.791 --> 00:21:29.751
Sumarah, the Javanese fellowship of total
surrender we looked at in Part 4,

353
00:21:29.751 --> 00:21:33.071
looks like the strongest card in the
secular dharma's hand,

354
00:21:33.171 --> 00:21:37.031
and, as a model, in some ways it is: a
living,

355
00:21:37.131 --> 00:21:43.091
non-Western tradition, now ninety years
old, that practises without scripture, guru,

356
00:21:43.091 --> 00:21:45.951
or creed; that asks nobody to convert,

357
00:21:45.971 --> 00:21:49.071
having nothing to convert them to, so that
Muslims,

358
00:21:49.191 --> 00:21:52.751
Christians and Buddhists sit together and
remain what they are;

359
00:21:52.951 --> 00:21:57.831
that tells its members to accept nothing
on faith ("We differ from religions,

360
00:21:57.831 --> 00:22:00.211
within which people accept God on faith
and

361
00:22:00.331 --> 00:22:03.051
without knowledge," its first chairman
said);

362
00:22:03.131 --> 00:22:07.251
and that installs, precisely, through the
"we" the modern seeker lacks,

363
00:22:07.651 --> 00:22:10.971
a weekly circle and a guide attuned to the
room,

364
00:22:10.971 --> 00:22:14.251
transformation by sitting together rather
than by doctrine.

365
00:22:14.791 --> 00:22:18.771
Most of what the three-part rule demands
was achieved in Java decades

366
00:22:18.771 --> 00:22:21.111
before any Western experiment began,

367
00:22:21.231 --> 00:22:25.511
and that is worth saying plainly: the
institutional half of the problem,

368
00:22:25.651 --> 00:22:28.891
practice without priesthood, and the
epistemological half,

369
00:22:29.211 --> 00:22:32.331
conviction without creed, are demonstrably
solvable,

370
00:22:32.371 --> 00:22:34.411
because a small fellowship solved them

371
00:22:34.411 --> 00:22:36.851
and has held the solution for three
generations.

372
00:22:37.541 --> 00:22:39.731
But the rest of the story cuts the other
way.

373
00:22:41.371 --> 00:22:46.011
When Sumarah's own practitioners devised a
religiously neutral "relaxed meditation"

374
00:22:46.011 --> 00:22:47.451
for officials and foreigners,

375
00:22:47.451 --> 00:22:49.791
the leadership ruled from inside that
meditation

376
00:22:49.791 --> 00:22:52.531
without God "led to void and not God";

377
00:22:52.871 --> 00:22:56.611
the apparatus was removable, but the
theism was the point.

378
00:22:57.231 --> 00:22:58.671
Even that ruling, though,

379
00:22:58.691 --> 00:23:02.611
has a shadow that could not be named while
the Indonesian New Order stood:

380
00:23:02.831 --> 00:23:05.031
it was issued under a state that had,

381
00:23:05.051 --> 00:23:09.991
after the massacres of 1965, treated
atheism as communism's twin --

382
00:23:10.251 --> 00:23:15.011
no statute ever named it; none was needed
to make it lethal to profess --

383
00:23:15.191 --> 00:23:18.951
and made priority to God the price of a
movement's legal existence,

384
00:23:19.251 --> 00:23:21.771
so how much of the floor was devotion,

385
00:23:22.171 --> 00:23:26.091
how much the pious etiquette that opens
all Indonesian public speech,

386
00:23:26.481 --> 00:23:29.951
and how much survival is a question the
record can no longer answer.

387
00:23:30.511 --> 00:23:34.671
The secular-feeling Sumarah that Western
practitioners sit in today exists

388
00:23:34.751 --> 00:23:38.591
because the translation was performed in
transit, by the receivers.

389
00:23:39.751 --> 00:23:44.371
A great deal, it turns out, can be stripped
from a dharma and the practice survive.

390
00:23:44.671 --> 00:23:47.631
What no tradition has yet shown, from the
inside,

391
00:23:47.651 --> 00:23:51.371
is the removal of its own God; that
operation, so far,

392
00:23:51.371 --> 00:23:54.751
has always been done by someone else,
somewhere downstream,

393
00:23:54.791 --> 00:23:58.351
and Sumarah, for three decades, was not
even free to try.

394
00:23:58.731 --> 00:24:01.771
The proof of concept is real, and it is
not ours.

395
00:24:02.711 --> 00:24:04.731
The hard part is still ours to do.

396
00:24:06.811 --> 00:24:09.131
So can a secular dharma install?

397
00:24:09.411 --> 00:24:11.871
The verdict is: sometimes,

398
00:24:11.891 --> 00:24:16.611
if it keeps the whole bundle and works hard
to replace what the gods used to supply.

399
00:24:16.931 --> 00:24:19.531
The secular experiments are real but
young,

400
00:24:19.531 --> 00:24:23.371
and we should be suspicious of how readily
the people running them,

401
00:24:23.371 --> 00:24:26.511
this project among them, declare their own
success.

402
00:24:26.951 --> 00:24:30.931
But the failure mode and the success mode
are now clear enough to steer by,

403
00:24:30.931 --> 00:24:32.331
and that is not nothing.

404
00:24:35.771 --> 00:24:39.691
The new terrain

405
00:24:39.691 --> 00:24:41.951
Suppose, though, that both problems were
solved,

406
00:24:42.191 --> 00:24:45.671
that a secular dharma could be both
grounded and installed.

407
00:24:46.601 --> 00:24:51.291
It would still have to do its work in a
world unlike any a dharma has faced before,

408
00:24:51.331 --> 00:24:54.431
and this is the part that is genuinely new
under the sun.

409
00:24:56.051 --> 00:24:59.271
Nearly every dharma in history was built
on the working assumption

410
00:24:59.271 --> 00:25:03.111
that the main obstacle to living well sat
inside your own skull:

411
00:25:03.111 --> 00:25:07.271
your own craving, your own distraction,
your own untrained mind.

412
00:25:07.601 --> 00:25:10.611
The contemplative traditions were,
accordingly,

413
00:25:10.651 --> 00:25:14.071
technologies for disciplining attention
against an inner enemy.

414
00:25:14.471 --> 00:25:18.791
The outer assault is not wholly new; the
last century's advertisers,

415
00:25:19.111 --> 00:25:22.071
propagandists and broadcasters had already
begun it.

416
00:25:22.531 --> 00:25:25.451
What is new, and only about twenty years
old,

417
00:25:25.451 --> 00:25:29.911
is an apparatus that is personalised,
ubiquitous, and self-improving:

418
00:25:30.091 --> 00:25:32.931
one that learns each mind's particular
weaknesses,

419
00:25:33.011 --> 00:25:37.791
individually and in real time, and gets
better at exploiting them with every use.

420
00:25:38.171 --> 00:25:42.391
The enemy is no longer only inside us, and
no longer generic.

421
00:25:42.971 --> 00:25:47.871
It is outside us, industrialised, very
well funded, and it knows you.

422
00:25:49.451 --> 00:25:51.951
We have built, in a single generation,

423
00:25:52.111 --> 00:25:54.891
what its own critics call the attention
economy,

424
00:25:55.091 --> 00:25:59.631
an enormous apparatus whose business is
the capture and resale of human attention.

425
00:26:00.131 --> 00:26:02.771
Our gaze is the product; the feed is the
bait.

426
00:26:03.191 --> 00:26:07.131
And the bait is engineered, with enormous
skill and oceans of data,

427
00:26:07.131 --> 00:26:10.471
to hijack the very reward systems
evolution gave us.

428
00:26:11.151 --> 00:26:13.571
In the psychologist Mark van Vugt's
description,

429
00:26:14.231 --> 00:26:16.751
social media is a supernormal stimulus,

430
00:26:17.051 --> 00:26:20.111
an exaggerated cue that feeds our ancient
hunger

431
00:26:20.111 --> 00:26:24.611
for connection far more cheaply and
intensely than real life ever could,

432
00:26:24.671 --> 00:26:28.811
the way junk food hijacks a palate evolved
for scarce sugar and fat.

433
00:26:29.171 --> 00:26:32.831
We have, he notes bleakly, no natural
defences against it.

434
00:26:35.121 --> 00:26:39.031
James Williams, who left a career inside
Google's advertising machine

435
00:26:39.031 --> 00:26:40.531
to study what it does to us,

436
00:26:40.531 --> 00:26:42.111
puts the stakes exactly:

437
00:26:42.271 --> 00:26:45.331
these systems are not neutral tools but
engines

438
00:26:45.331 --> 00:26:49.791
for "redirecting people away from their
wills," from what they actually,

439
00:26:49.791 --> 00:26:52.271
reflectively want their lives to be.

440
00:26:52.691 --> 00:26:56.731
A dharma's oldest and deepest job was to
hold a human life together,

441
00:26:56.751 --> 00:26:59.371
to gather a scattered self around what
matters.

442
00:27:00.071 --> 00:27:03.051
The attention economy is, almost
precisely,

443
00:27:03.251 --> 00:27:06.871
a machine built for the opposite purpose:
to scatter the self,

444
00:27:07.031 --> 00:27:09.571
to shatter attention into sellable
fragments,

445
00:27:09.711 --> 00:27:13.311
to keep us reactive and outraged and never
quite still.

446
00:27:15.111 --> 00:27:17.431
Private attention is only half the damage;

447
00:27:17.591 --> 00:27:20.691
the rest falls on the shared world we
attend to together.

448
00:27:21.111 --> 00:27:24.471
The old gatekeepers of information, for
all their faults,

449
00:27:24.551 --> 00:27:28.511
are gone, and into the gap pours an
undifferentiated flood.

450
00:27:29.011 --> 00:27:31.291
The best-known study of how falsehood
moves

451
00:27:31.291 --> 00:27:33.991
through a social network found it
spreading further

452
00:27:33.991 --> 00:27:35.691
and faster than the truth,

453
00:27:35.811 --> 00:27:40.371
and the usual explanation is that outrage
is the most engaging product of all.

454
00:27:40.731 --> 00:27:43.911
I would not lean the whole weight of an
argument on that finding,

455
00:27:43.911 --> 00:27:46.631
which concerns one platform in one period
and

456
00:27:46.691 --> 00:27:49.591
which later researchers have worked hard
to qualify.

457
00:27:51.261 --> 00:27:53.471
But you do not need the strong version.

458
00:27:53.751 --> 00:27:57.911
It is enough that the sorting is done by
engagement rather than by truth,

459
00:27:57.911 --> 00:28:00.651
and that nobody designed it to care about
the difference.

460
00:28:01.261 --> 00:28:05.811
Worse, the very act of publicly broadcasting
a conviction tends to harden it,

461
00:28:05.851 --> 00:28:09.391
so a medium built for endless broadcasting
is also a machine

462
00:28:09.391 --> 00:28:12.411
for making everyone more certain and less
movable.

463
00:28:12.791 --> 00:28:16.191
The new age does not tame the failure
modes traced earlier;

464
00:28:16.191 --> 00:28:18.351
it amplifies and accelerates them.

465
00:28:18.791 --> 00:28:21.571
The purity spiral that once took a
generation

466
00:28:21.571 --> 00:28:25.091
to consume a community can now run its
course in a weekend,

467
00:28:25.491 --> 00:28:27.271
and the lethal fence between "us"

468
00:28:27.271 --> 00:28:30.391
and "them" is rebuilt a thousand times a
day by systems

469
00:28:30.391 --> 00:28:33.631
that have quietly learned that division
holds attention better than peace.

470
00:28:34.251 --> 00:28:38.651
A dharma for this terrain is not facing
the old, slow drift of a wandering mind.

471
00:28:39.271 --> 00:28:42.771
It is facing an adversary that gets
cleverer every quarter.

472
00:28:44.511 --> 00:28:48.131
This forces something new on any dharma
that means to survive here.

473
00:28:48.591 --> 00:28:51.291
The old contemplative move, work on the
self,

474
00:28:51.371 --> 00:28:54.851
train one's own mind, is necessary but no
longer sufficient,

475
00:28:55.251 --> 00:28:58.791
because no amount of private inner
discipline can hold out indefinitely

476
00:28:58.811 --> 00:29:02.571
against a billion-dollar industry
engineering our weaknesses in real time.

477
00:29:03.211 --> 00:29:07.131
A digital-age dharma has to work on the
environment as well as the self:

478
00:29:07.191 --> 00:29:10.371
on the design of the machines, on the
rules that govern them,

479
00:29:10.371 --> 00:29:12.211
on the shared structures of attention.

480
00:29:14.251 --> 00:29:17.711
The contemplative traditions were never
hermetically apolitical;

481
00:29:17.811 --> 00:29:22.191
monasteries negotiated with kings, and
patronage shaped what could be taught.

482
00:29:23.011 --> 00:29:24.871
But no path before this one had

483
00:29:24.871 --> 00:29:27.671
to treat the design of the surrounding
attention environment

484
00:29:27.671 --> 00:29:29.471
as itself a matter of practice,

485
00:29:29.711 --> 00:29:33.611
and that political and collective register
is now central rather than marginal.

486
00:29:34.171 --> 00:29:37.651
The new register is audible in the people
groping toward it:

487
00:29:37.711 --> 00:29:39.471
the writer Jenny Odell's call

488
00:29:39.471 --> 00:29:42.951
to resist the colonisation of our
attention by reattaching it

489
00:29:42.951 --> 00:29:45.651
to place and community and the physical
world,

490
00:29:45.911 --> 00:29:48.831
or the movements pressing for a more
humane technology

491
00:29:48.831 --> 00:29:52.231
that would be built to serve our
intentions rather than to farm them.

492
00:29:54.271 --> 00:29:59.511
And the deepest irony of all: the single
most celebrated secular dharma of our time,

493
00:29:59.511 --> 00:30:04.111
mindfulness, has itself been swallowed
whole by the attention economy,

494
00:30:04.331 --> 00:30:08.251
sold back to us as the very coping tool
that lets us endure the machine,

495
00:30:08.331 --> 00:30:10.811
the same hollowing McMindfulness names.

496
00:30:11.431 --> 00:30:13.971
The cure has been digested by the disease.

497
00:30:14.491 --> 00:30:18.391
Any real dharma for this age has to be more
than a better way of putting up with it.

498
00:30:20.031 --> 00:30:21.991
One thing does not change in all this,

499
00:30:22.151 --> 00:30:25.131
and it points to the resource a new dharma
has to draw on.

500
00:30:25.631 --> 00:30:29.071
The deepest contemplative traditions never
treated attention

501
00:30:29.071 --> 00:30:32.231
as a neutral spotlight to aim wherever one
pleased.

502
00:30:33.031 --> 00:30:37.471
In Buddhism, the relevant idea is not
"bare attention" but something closer

503
00:30:37.471 --> 00:30:41.371
to "appropriate attention," a noticing
already shaped by ethics

504
00:30:41.371 --> 00:30:44.951
and intention and woven into the whole
question of how to live.

505
00:30:45.691 --> 00:30:47.051
The attention economy,

506
00:30:47.051 --> 00:30:50.991
by reducing mindfulness to a content-free
knack of just-noticing,

507
00:30:51.071 --> 00:30:53.311
severed attention from that ethical core,

508
00:30:53.311 --> 00:30:57.291
and that severing is exactly what made it
so easy to capture and resell.

509
00:30:59.231 --> 00:31:02.931
A real digital-age dharma is therefore
partly a recovery,

510
00:31:02.931 --> 00:31:04.791
the same move Hadot described:

511
00:31:04.891 --> 00:31:08.551
it restores the old truth that what you
attend to, and how,

512
00:31:08.551 --> 00:31:13.031
is already a moral act, the very fabric of
the person you are slowly becoming.

513
00:31:13.591 --> 00:31:16.351
And it has begun to grow its own concrete
practices.

514
00:31:17.231 --> 00:31:19.691
The writer Cal Newport's "digital
minimalism" is

515
00:31:19.691 --> 00:31:22.711
recognisably an installation practice in
the old sense:

516
00:31:22.871 --> 00:31:24.371
a month-long declutter,

517
00:31:24.411 --> 00:31:26.931
a deliberate, almost ruthless pruning of
the apps

518
00:31:26.931 --> 00:31:29.891
and feeds back down to the few that serve
a chosen value,

519
00:31:30.071 --> 00:31:34.751
a ritual of subtraction meant to re-train
a scattered attention until focus becomes,

520
00:31:34.751 --> 00:31:38.571
once again, a settled disposition rather
than a daily losing fight.

521
00:31:41.561 --> 00:31:43.331
The shape of the question changes

522
00:31:45.811 --> 00:31:47.811
The inherited dharmas are fraying,

523
00:31:47.811 --> 00:31:50.311
and most of them were made for a world
that is gone.

524
00:31:51.131 --> 00:31:55.331
The secular replacements can perhaps be
grounded, though only in the quieter,

525
00:31:55.331 --> 00:31:58.571
conditional way that may be the only
grounding there ever was;

526
00:31:58.871 --> 00:32:00.791
and they can perhaps be installed,

527
00:32:00.831 --> 00:32:04.391
though the apparatus is improvised and the
evidence is young;

528
00:32:04.731 --> 00:32:07.091
and they must do their work on terrain
more hostile

529
00:32:07.211 --> 00:32:10.291
to the gathering of a self than anything
in human history.

530
00:32:10.771 --> 00:32:12.311
That is a daunting tally.

531
00:32:13.011 --> 00:32:14.751
But the seeker we started with,

532
00:32:14.751 --> 00:32:18.091
the one with the yoga and the app and the
book of Stoic sayings,

533
00:32:18.091 --> 00:32:19.131
looks different now.

534
00:32:19.291 --> 00:32:21.451
She is not a symptom of decline.

535
00:32:21.691 --> 00:32:25.751
She is doing, alone and clumsily and
mostly without help,

536
00:32:25.751 --> 00:32:27.871
the most ancient human work there is:

537
00:32:28.171 --> 00:32:32.751
trying to assemble, out of the materials
at hand, a dharma she can live by.

538
00:32:33.371 --> 00:32:34.691
The patchwork is construction,

539
00:32:35.271 --> 00:32:38.691
under the worst conditions in which the
work has ever been attempted.

540
00:32:40.471 --> 00:32:42.691
And that recognition changes the very
shape

541
00:32:42.691 --> 00:32:44.971
of the question we have been asking all
along.

542
00:32:45.671 --> 00:32:49.731
For all of human history before now, the
question a person faced was:

543
00:32:49.951 --> 00:32:54.431
which of the inherited dharmas is the true
one, and how do I submit myself to it?

544
00:32:55.091 --> 00:32:58.671
One was born onto a path, and the task was
to walk it.

545
00:32:59.251 --> 00:33:03.031
But the seeker cannot ask that question
any more, and she knows it.

546
00:33:03.601 --> 00:33:07.131
So the question is mutating, in her hands
and in ours,

547
00:33:07.331 --> 00:33:11.111
into something no previous age could quite
bring itself to ask out loud.

548
00:33:11.831 --> 00:33:16.011
Not which dharma do I receive, but: can we
build a better one,

549
00:33:16.211 --> 00:33:18.791
knowing exactly what we are doing, on
purpose?

550
00:33:20.731 --> 00:33:22.651
That is a vertiginous question.

551
00:33:23.111 --> 00:33:25.711
It is also the most dangerous question
there is,

552
00:33:25.751 --> 00:33:29.011
because the instant one says out loud that
dharmas can be built,

553
00:33:29.091 --> 00:33:33.091
the terrible follow-ups arrive: built by
whom, for whom,

554
00:33:33.091 --> 00:33:36.051
toward what end, and what gets crushed in
the building?

555
00:33:36.611 --> 00:33:40.991
And we are being made to ask it at the
most demanding moment imaginable,

556
00:33:41.051 --> 00:33:45.631
on a planet in crisis that may need some
shared ethic to survive at all,

557
00:33:45.631 --> 00:33:49.991
and on the eve of building machines that
may soon think, into which someone,

558
00:33:49.991 --> 00:33:54.511
somewhere, is already preparing to pour an
ethic of their own.

559
00:33:54.991 --> 00:33:57.911
We have come, at last, to the making of
dharmas.

560
00:33:58.551 --> 00:33:59.695
That is Part 8.

